The comment line to disable the postcss-lit plugin (`postcss-lit-disable-next-line`) doesn't necessarily need to be on the parent statement, especially with static class fields being more and more mainstream: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Classes/static

The following snippet breaks stylelint with postcss-lit:

```javascript
const MY_CONST = 600;

export class MyClass extends LitElement {
  static styles = css`
    @media (width >= ${MY_CONST}px) {
      ...
    }
  `;
}
```

Errors:
- Unexpected invalid media query        media-query-no-invalid
   "(width >= POSTCSS_LIT_0px)"
- Unexpected unknown media feature      media-feature-name-no-unknown
   name "POSTCSS_LIT_0px"

To disable the plugin, I'd now have to disable it for the entire class because `styles` is a static class field and therefore the parent statement is the `MyClass` class:
```javascript
const MY_CONST = 600;

// postcss-lit-disable-next-line
export class MyClass extends LitElement {
  static styles = css`
    @media (width >= ${MY_CONST}px) {
      ...
    }
  `;
}
```

Because my classes contain more CSS than just this media statement, I'd like to be able to not disable this plugin for the entire class nor do I want to replace my static class field back to a getter. This PR allows disable comments to be placed directly above the statement, but the previous behaviour of looking at the parent comments is taking precedence so that it should not be a breaking change. This now works:

```javascript
const MY_CONST = 600;

export class MyClass extends LitElement {
  static styles = [
    // postcss-lit-disable-next-line
    css`
      @media (width >= ${MY_CONST}px) {
        ...
      }
    `,
    css`
      .my-class-i-want-to-stylelint {
        ...
      }
    `,
  ];
}
```

I also made it so the disable comment doesn't necessarily need to be the last comment before the statement. It can be one of the comments which is required if I also want to disable for example eslint for the same line, e.g.:

```javascript
// postcss-lit-disable-next-line
// eslint-disable-next-line
css`
  @media (width >= ${MY_CONST}px) {
    ...
  }
`
```

Let me know what you think, I'm open for suggestions!

---

EDIT:
Future work: maybe think about also supporting the following syntax:

```javascript
css`
  /* postcss-lit-disable-next-line */
  .foo { color: hotpink; }
`;
```

As far as I can see the CSS tagged template expression is not parsed by Babel though, so it might require using another library for that.

`hal::can` does not support filters and trying to add support to that singular interface has been challenging. Not only that, but it puts a ton of responsibility on the singular interface and will make interacting with can drivers problematic. 

Consider the fact that a can driver that only wants to send messages also has control over the bus baud rate. But just allowing higher privilege APIs to mere drivers isn't the real problem. its that we'd have to have a set of complicated rules about which users of the singular can interface can do what and at what time. 

Finally, the kind and number of filters available to each device is strongly variable. Adding interfaces for each type of filter interface would bloat the vtable of `hal::can` and would make interfacing with such APIs complicated. For example, if a CAN implementation only provides a single mask filter, what happens if we call the API on the interface to set a range filter? Should it throw? Should it return the bool value false to indicate that this resource is not supported? That sounds complicated and adds a bunch of additional branches and error handling code. A simpler approach would be separate the concerns across abstracts and use whats available.

Driver and applications request what filters they need and its up to the developer to acquire these from the hardware OR wrap a filter in a sort of software converter that makes the mask available for the driver or application. For example:

```C++
hal::can_mask_filter& mask_filter = get_mask_filter();
hal::can_mask_to_identifier_filter id_filter(mask_filter;
my_can_driver driver(id_filter);
```

The `can_mask_to_identifier_filter` would implement an allow filter that passes the ID filter to the mask filter where the mask is `0x7FF` (signaling that all bits must match exactly)

